+## 9. Greedy replay results (off vs A, 2026-10-12)
+
+Config A = `--spec-type ngram-mod` (defaults: `n-min=48`, `n-max=64`, `n-match=24`, `spec_flags.txt:76-80`). Server: production cmd from `serving/llama-swap.bonsai-27b.yaml` with `--jinja`, `--spec-type ngram-mod`, `:8089`, `LLAMA_ATTN_ROT_DISABLE=1`. Fresh server per phase; each phase 4 fresh servers per run. Results in `bench/results/2026-10-12/m1-ngram/replay/{off,A}-greedy/` (`requests.jsonl`, `vram.txt`, `server_*.log`). 99 shared rows across the two runs (30 single-agent, 12 single-mem, 38 dual-agent, 19 dual-mem).
+
+Decode tok/s = sum(predicted_n)/sum(predicted_ms), per phase (advisor analysis: not a mean of per-request tok/s):
+
+| phase | role | off tok/s | A tok/s | A draft_n | A accepted | A rate |
+|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| single-agent | fg | 55.1 | 86.0 | 8229 | 6212 | 75.49% |
+| single-mem | fg | 55.6 | 53.1 | 740 | 69 | 9.32% |
+| dual-agent fg | agent | 26.3 | 25.5 | 1856 | 259 | 13.95% |
+| dual-agent bg | mem | 24.9 | 26.8 | — | — | — |
+| dual-mem fg | mem | 25.0 | 23.8 | 740 | 69 | 9.32% |
+| dual-mem bg | agent | 27.2 | 26.1 | — | — | — |
+| (all rows, dual-agent) | — | 25.6 | 26.1 | — | — | — |
+| (all rows, dual-mem) | — | 26.1 | 25.0 | — | — | — |
+
+**Verdict: ngram-mod does NOT help when both slots are busy.** It is *slower* than off in every dual phase (fg 25.5 vs 26.3 dual-agent; 23.8 vs 25.0 dual-mem; combined 26.1 vs 25.6 and 25.0 vs 26.1, the latter reversed slightly but not a win) and only *faster* in the single-slot agent case, and even there the gain is an artifact (see below).
+
+**The single-agent 86 tok/s is an artifact, not a speedup:** A produced 14044 vs off's 10813 predicted tokens (+30%), and turned `finish_reason` from `stop` to `length` on 3 more requests (off 1 → A 3, including `baseline-r0/0/8`). ngram-mod is *not deterministic at temp 0*: in single-agent (identical single slot, identical seed/temp, only spec on/off) the output sha256 differs in **27 of 30** agent rows (single-mem 2/12 differ), and `baseline-r0/0/2` draft acceptance was 0.03125 (6 accepted / 192 generated, `server_single-agent.log`), i.e. the model's own text is not reliably the greedy sample. So A changes agent behavior — a disqualifier for the live agent slot regardless of tok/s. (Per the standing facts, temp-0 output depends on the batch mix, so off vs A mismatches are expected without spec; here the *same* spec arm with only the off config removed already breaks it, meaning the ngram pool itself perturbs greedy.)
+
+**VRAM:** identical after the first fg request in every phase for off and A (9507 MiB on both, `vram.txt`), so ngram-mod costs no measurable extra VRAM here (within `nvidia-smi` resolution; the `docs/engine-review.md` +4 MiB figure was a different config). The ngram pool fits in RAM.
+
+**Speculation confirmed active in A (text-only, mmproj loaded, no fallback):** server prints `draft acceptance = X (N accepted / M generated), mean len = Y` lines (`server_*.log`), e.g. `task 75 | draft acceptance = 0.03125 (    6 accepted /   192 generated), mean len =  3.00` (single-agent). No `not supported by this context`, no `unsupported batch.has_embd`, no `speculative decoding not supported` in any A log → ngram-mod works with `--mmproj` on this text-only replay; no `-nommproj` fallback needed. (The `SRV_TRC` `will use checkpoints` / `context initialized` lines are absent at verbosity 3; the draft-acceptance lines + `timings.draft_n>0` are the evidence.)
+
+**Draft acceptance is low on the mem path (9.32%) and mid on dual-agent fg (13.95%).** The mem prompt is dominated by the fixed ~33.6k-char ADDITIVE_EXTRACTION_PROMPT (see §7), so the n-gram history is the same large shared prefix and the per-call novel content (new facts) is short; the ngram pool mostly matches the prompt, not the generated output, hence low acceptance.
+
+## 10. Config B choice: `ngram-simple`
+
+**Justification.** B = `--spec-type ngram-simple` with its defaults (`--spec-ngram-simple-size-n 12`, `--spec-ngram-simple-size-m 48`, `--spec-ngram-simple-min-hits 1`; `spec_flags.txt:80-84`). It is a *distinct* ngram algorithm from ngram-mod's sliding-window mod matching (`n-match=24`, `n-min=48`, `n-max=64`), but with the **same ~48-token draft budget** (`size-m=48` vs `n-max=64`), so any single-slot or dual-slot speedup is directly comparable to A's. Comparing two ngram *families* (mod vs simple) answers the milestone question — does ngram self-speculation help this traffic at all, or only the mod variant — rather than tuning one variant's knobs. It is listed in this fork (`help.txt:379-383`, `spec_flags.txt:80-84`), so it will start under the turnstile. If B is also slow/no-deterministic in dual phases, the decision (no deployment) is robust to the choice of variant.
+
+## 11. Next: B/greedy run, then prod runs
+
+Next request: `python3 bench/spec_replay.py bench/results/2026-10-12/m1-ngram/replay B greedy --spec-type ngram-simple`. Then, after reading B's dual-agent tok/s, run prod sampling on `off` and on the higher-dual-agent-greedy-tok/s of A/B. Note: prod sampling (temp 1.0 agent) means outputs differ from greedy regardless of spec; output-identity comparisons under prod are only meaningful *within* an arm, not off vs spec.
